<article class="env-lemma" id="094X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/094X">Lemma <span data-tag="094X">37.64.9</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes. Then $X \to Y$ is weakly étale in each of the following cases </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X \to Y$ is a flat monomorphism, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X \to Y$ is an open immersion, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X \to Y$ is flat and unramified, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X \to Y$ is étale. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If (1) holds, then $\Delta _{X/Y}$ is an isomorphism (Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01L3" data-tag="01L3">26.23.2</a>), hence certainly $f$ is weakly étale. Case (2) is a special case of (1). The diagonal of an unramified morphism is an open immersion (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/02GE" data-tag="02GE">29.36.13</a>), hence flat. Thus a flat unramified morphism is weakly étale. An étale morphism is flat and unramified (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/02GK" data-tag="02GK">29.37.5</a>), hence (4) follows from (3). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>